            <ArticleTitle>Isoindigo-3,4-Difluorothiophene Polymer Acceptors Yield &quot;All-Polymer&quot; Bulk-Heterojunction Solar Cells with over 7 % Efficiency.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>531-535</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/anie.201709509</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Poly(isoindigo-alt-3,4-difluorothiophene) (PIID[2F]T) analogues used as &quot;polymer acceptors&quot; in bulk-heterojunction (BHJ) solar cells achieve &gt;7 % efficiency when used in conjunction with the polymer donor PBFTAZ (model system; copolymer of benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene and 5,6-difluorobenzotriazole). Considering that most efficient polymer-acceptor alternatives to fullerenes (e.g. PC61 BM or its C71 derivative) are based on perylenediimide or naphthalenediimide motifs thus far, branched alkyl-substituted PIID[2F]T polymers are particularly promising non-fullerene candidates for &quot;all-polymer&quot; BHJ solar cells.</AbstractText>
